
🔹 System Call
A system call is a way for a user program to request a service from the Operating System (OS kernel).

User programs cannot directly access hardware (for protection & security).

So they call OS services (like file handling, process control, networking) using system calls.

✨ Examples:
File operations → open(), read(), write(), close()

Process control → fork(), exec(), exit()

Device management → requesting I/O device usage

Memory management → malloc() internally calls brk() or mmap()

👉 In Linux, when you write:

printf("Hello");
It eventually triggers the write() system call to send data to screen.

-------------------------------------
User Program (printf/write)
        │
        ▼
C Library (glibc wrapper)
        │
       Call Dispatcher
        │
        ▼
Specific System Call Handler (e.g., sys_write)
        │
        ▼
Device Driver / Hardware
        │
        ▼
Return Value → Back to User Space
        │   (switch: Kernel Mode → User Mode)
        ▼
User Program continues

