🔹 Interrupt
An interrupt is a signal to the CPU that an event needs immediate attention.

It stops current execution, saves CPU state, and transfers control to an Interrupt Service Routine (ISR).

After handling, CPU resumes where it left off.

✨ Types of Interrupts:
Hardware Interrupts – from external devices

Example: Keyboard key press, Mouse click, Network packet arrival.

Software Interrupts – triggered by programs (e.g., divide by zero error, system call).

Timer Interrupts – generated by system clock to manage multitasking.

🔹 Relationship between System Calls & Interrupts
A system call is actually implemented using a software interrupt.

Example (x86 Linux):

User program executes int 0x80 or syscall instruction.

CPU switches from User Mode → Kernel Mode.

Jumps to system call handler in kernel.

OS executes the requested service.

Control returns to user program.
